I"<p>A model learning to solve algorithm problems can do well on the input lengths it sees during training and stumble on the lengths in between. An engineer might inspect those failures and add a computation that handles the missing case. DoubleAI asks what happens when a coding agent can do that work while the model is still training. In one reported experiment, adding agent-written code raised accuracy on those intermediate lengths from 33% to 79%. <a href="https://www.doubleai.com/research/can-llms-redesign-themselves-as-they-train#taking-on-jaggedness">DoubleAI’s research article</a></p>

<!--more-->

<h2 id="the-agent-diagnoses-a-failure-and-proposes-a-change">The agent diagnoses a failure and proposes a change</h2>

<p>DoubleAI calls this approach <strong>mechanistic intervention</strong>. The agent inspects a model’s internal representations and looks for recurring errors. One diagnostic, called a <em>phantom gradient</em>, measures how a small change to an internal representation would affect the loss. A recurring signal can suggest where a correction could help. The agent still has to work out what computation would produce it. <a href="https://www.doubleai.com/research/can-llms-redesign-themselves-as-they-train#nanochat-where-does-a-language-model-need-help">Nanochat experiment and diagnostics</a></p>

<p>Imagine a model trying to keep track of the largest number in a list. A useful intervention might read the numbers from the model’s internal representation, calculate the largest value seen so far, and return that result to a later layer. This splits the work into three pieces. The model supplies a representation, a program performs the operation, and the network learns how to use the result. In DoubleAI’s experiments, a running maximum is one example of a computation a code layer can supply. <a href="https://www.doubleai.com/research/can-llms-redesign-themselves-as-they-train#taking-on-jaggedness">Code layers on CLRS-Text</a></p>

<h2 id="how-the-code-enters-the-model">How the code enters the model</h2>

<p>In the training experiments, a <strong>code layer</strong> is an extra branch in the model’s forward pass. It runs alongside the existing computation and adds a result to the information that flows to later layers. A learnable multiplier, called a <em>gate</em>, scales that addition. It starts at zero, so the new branch contributes nothing at first. As training resumes, the optimizer can change how much the branch contributes. <a href="https://www.doubleai.com/research/can-llms-redesign-themselves-as-they-train#nanochat-where-does-a-language-model-need-help">Code layer integration</a></p>

<p>The gate gives two kinds of changes their own roles. The agent proposes a discrete edit by writing a program and choosing where to connect it. Training adjusts numerical weights, including the gate. DoubleAI compares candidates with runs from the same checkpoint and with controls that scramble the information a branch carries. Those comparisons help separate the benefit of the computation from the benefit of giving the model extra capacity. <a href="https://www.doubleai.com/research/can-llms-redesign-themselves-as-they-train#nanochat-where-does-a-language-model-need-help">Candidate comparisons</a></p>

