<h2 id="choosing-what-the-model-can-compute">Choosing what the model can compute</h2>

<p>In my earlier post, <a href="/machine%20learning/computer%20science/computation-inside-language-models/">Computation Inside Language Models</a>, the examples mostly started with a procedure and asked where it could run. DoubleAI starts with an observed failure and asks what procedure should be added, where to connect it, and when to introduce it. The lookup experiment makes the timing question concrete. The same operation produced different recall depending on when the model acquired it.</p>

<p>The next question is whether a system can reuse what it learns about intervention. A tool that finds a bottleneck in one model might help diagnose another. To test that possibility, I would track which diagnostic methods transfer across tasks and architectures, and count the cost of the agent’s search along with the cost of training the model it changes.</p>
